  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nCurrently, the ridge axis is shifting back west as the blocking \npattern reemerges with a trough across the Pacific Northwest and a \ntrough digging into the Northeast CONUS. Heights remain steady over \nthe region, though, continuing the abnormally warm conditions but \nnot leading to further warming. At the surface, a weak surface \ntrough extends across the Central High Plains in the lee of the \nRockies, and a remnant MCV lingers over southwest and central Kansas \nfrom a storm complex last evening. A few clouds will linger over the \nregion today, but otherwise few impacts are expected with this \nfeature. There remains a very low probability (less than 10% chance) \nthat an isolated shower or thunderstorm could initiate with diurnal \nheating this afternoon in conjunction with the remnant boundary, but \nthat appears unlikely. Highs today will once again reach into the \nupper 90s and lower 100s areawide. Later this afternoon/evening, \nstorms are expected to once again form in eastern Colorado and \nprogress eastward into western Kansas. This activity could creep \ninto our central Kansas counties by early Sunday morning. Then on \nSunday, a slow moving boundary associated with the trough over the \nNortheast US will be draped across the Middle Mississippi Valley. A \nfew showers and storms could develop along this boundary on Sunday \nafternoon and impact our far southeastern Kansas counties into the \nevening hours.\n\nThe ridge will remain overhead through early week. Aside from the \naforementioned isolated precip chances, we are expected to remain \nmostly dry. Temperatures through Tuesday will top out right around \n100 degrees for all locations across the forecast area with heat \nindices up to 105 degrees. The Heat Advisory remains in effect \nthrough 8PM Tuesday. By Wednesday, the trough currently over the \nPacific Northwest is forecast to become more progressive and begin \nto traverse the northern tier of the CONUS. If this model trend \ncontinues, this will act to displace the ridge further south and \neast. The associated surface low over the southern Canadian Prairies \nwould then drag a frontal boundary across the Plains by mid week. If \nthis solution comes to pass, slightly cooler temperatures and \nincreased rain chances may be in play for late next week.",
